#!/bin/sh
# Refuse a push whose tree does not lint and build.
#
# # Why this exists
#
# On 2026-09-07 a commit was pushed with a lint failure in it. The cause was not
# carelessness about lint — it was that the check and the push were chained with
# ";" instead of "&&", so a failing gate did not stop the command that followed.
# CI caught it a minute later and the fix went out as a second push.
#
# The obvious answer was branch protection with required status checks. That was
# considered and refused: on GitHub required checks gate PR MERGES, so making the
# push genuinely depend on CI means main stops accepting direct pushes and every
# change goes through a pull request. This repository has forty-odd direct
# commits on main and one author; that is ceremony bought with somebody else's
# time to fix a mistake that belongs to the person making it.
#
# So the guard is put where the mistake happens instead. It runs on every push,
# it cannot be forgotten, and it costs about a minute.
#
# # What it does NOT run
#
# Not the tests. `make test` is the race-and-coverage lane and takes minutes;
# integration and smoke need Docker and take longer still. A guard that makes
# every push expensive is a guard people learn to skip with --no-verify, and a
# guard that is always skipped is worse than none because it reads as protection.
# Lint and build are what catch the class of failure this was: a tree that does
# not compile, or that trips a linter nobody ran.
#
# CI remains the authority. This only stops the push that was never going to pass.
#
# To bypass deliberately:  git push --no-verify
# To turn it off:          git config --unset core.hooksPath
set -e

printf 'pre-push: building...\n'
go build ./...

printf 'pre-push: linting...\n'
make lint

printf 'pre-push: ok\n'
